<description>
Fill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.
- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,
  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).
- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;
  their gap defines the step.
- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a
  repeating color.

Rule:
- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.
- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;
  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.
- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is
  a companion: it absorbs the ray and becomes the fill color, limiting the
  fill range to the span between repeating and companion dots.
- An unaligned dot is a bystander and left untouched.
- Without a companion the repeating color fills the entire line.

Key insight: the shooting edge is identified by counting boundary dots;
the gap between the two marker dots is the period; a companion sharing the
modular class acts as a wall that stops the ray and dictates the paint color.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "sparse colored dots",
  "path": "within each affected row/col, step size = gcd of gaps between repeated dots; traverse indices congruent to the first dot modulo step",
  "trajectory": "paint with the aligned companion color if present on the same modulo-class; otherwise paint with the dot color",
  "dst": "fill the line segment between extreme occurrences (or whole line if no companion color)",
  "transform": "fill background/marker cells on that path with the trajectory color; ties apply both row and column rules"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 7-12 rows x 9-21 cols (output same size as input)
  background: 0 (most common color)
  shooting_edge: exactly one of top/bottom/left/right, identified by having strictly more non-bg dots on that boundary than any other
  direction: left/right edges -> rows, top/bottom edges -> columns
  active_lines: 4-9 lines containing non-bg dots; remaining lines are all-bg
  active_line_spacing: gaps >= 1 between active line indices [observed]
  per_active_line:
    marker: exactly 1 repeating color with exactly 2 dots; one dot is on the shooting edge (pos 0 or line_len-1), the other is interior; step = gap between them (range 1-5)
    companion: 0 or 1 different-colored single dot aligned to the marker beat (pos % step == offset); always beyond the inner marker dot (away from edge); limits fill range and overrides paint color
    bystander: 0 or 1 different-colored single dot NOT aligned to the marker beat; left unchanged in output
    total_dots_per_line: 2 (marker only) or 3 (marker + companion or bystander)
  output_rule:
    no_companion: fill every beat position (pos % step == offset) across the entire line with the marker color; marker dots become paint color
    with_companion: fill beat positions only within the span [min, max] of marker + companion positions using the companion color; marker dots within span become companion color
    bystanders: unchanged
  colors: 2-8 distinct non-bg colors per grid; marker colors may repeat across lines; a color can serve as marker on one line and companion on another

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid dims and shooting edge; set line_len accordingly
    2. Choose shooting_edge and 4-9 active line indices with gaps >= 1
    3. For each active line, pick a marker color, step (1-5), and decide companion vs no-companion
    4. Build the output line (filled beats across full line or within companion-bounded span)
    5. Derive input by keeping only the 2 marker dots (edge + edge+step or edge-step) plus optional companion dot and optional unaligned bystander dot; erase all other filled positions to bg
    6. Verify shooting edge is unambiguous (its dot count strictly exceeds all other edges)
```
</generation_spec>
